WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Privacy expectations become real through technical and organizational safeguards.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:28.200
Identity and access management, least privilege, role-based access, privileged-access management, multifactor authentication, separation of duties, tenant isolation, network segmentation, secure interfaces, encryption, key management, secrets management, secure development, change control, vulnerability management, monitoring, and secure deletion are all relevant.

3
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.660
The point is not to collect impressive control names.

4
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:39.280
The point is to implement controls that address identified threats and privacy risks for the scoped cloud service.

5
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.240
Each major safeguard should be described in operational terms.

6
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.340
What objective does it serve?

7
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.240
Which threat or privacy risk does it reduce?

8
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:55.220
Which service, data category, region, tenant, or support process is in scope?

9
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.680
Who owns the control?

10
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.780
Which configuration implements it?

11
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.480
Which exceptions exist?

12
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:01.780
How is it monitored?

13
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.080
How is it tested?

14
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.060
Which evidence demonstrates operation?

15
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.920
How often is it reviewed?

16
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.780
Which customer dependency affects whether the control is effective?

17
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.640
These questions separate control design from control operation.

18
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.320
Access control illustrates the difference.

19
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:33.860
A provider may state that access is restricted, but effectiveness depends on identity lifecycle, privileged access approval, support-session controls, logging, periodic review, separation of duties, emergency access handling, and removal of stale access.

20
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.760
Customer-controlled identities add another boundary.

21
00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:48.140
If the customer creates broad administrator accounts or disables multifactor authentication where it is available, the provider's service controls may not protect the processing activity as intended.

22
00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.180
Encryption is another example.

23
00:01:50.280 --> 00:02:07.480
Encryption in transit and at rest can reduce exposure, but key ownership, key rotation, key access, backup encryption, logging of key events, customer-managed versus provider-managed keys, and recovery procedures determine the actual assurance value.

24
00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:16.940
Encryption does not resolve unauthorized purpose, excessive retention, unclear subprocessor use, or misuse of support access.

25
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.960
It is a powerful safeguard, not a complete privacy program.

26
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:47.000
Privacy by design in cloud engineering means reducing unnecessary PII, reducing unnecessary copies, using safe defaults, separating tenants, restricting administrative paths, making access observable, designing deletion into storage architecture, avoiding PII in diagnostics, controlling data used for development and testing, and planning customer export and termination before the first enterprise customer asks.

27
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:54.300
It also includes regional processing boundaries when those boundaries are part of customer commitments or applicable obligations.

28
00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:58.020
Organizational controls matter as much as technical configuration.

29
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:12.440
Personnel screening where appropriate, confidentiality commitments, awareness, training, support procedures, incident playbooks, change-management review, secure development practices, and corrective-action tracking all influence PII protection.

30
00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:21.340
A secure interface can still be misused if support staff lack procedures, and a strong policy can still fail if no system logs the activity.

31
00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.780
The review standard is effectiveness.

32
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:30.360
The existence of a control does not prove it is operating, and evidence of one test does not prove continuing assurance.

33
00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:40.140
A provider should be able to connect control objective, implementation, monitoring, test results, exceptions, remediation, and customer-facing commitments.

34
00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:51.040
A customer should evaluate not only whether the provider lists controls, but whether those controls are scoped, operated, reviewed, and supported by evidence that matches the service being purchased.

35
00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.600
Cloud safeguards also have interaction effects.

36
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.940
Strong tenant isolation can be weakened by an overly broad support role.

37
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.680
Encryption at rest can be undermined by poor key administration.

38
00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.060
Secure development can be undercut by unmanaged test data.

39
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:12.840
Monitoring can create privacy exposure if diagnostics capture payloads unnecessarily.

40
00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:19.340
A sound design reviews the control set as a system rather than as independent boxes.

41
00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:28.960
The result should be a coherent operating model in which preventive, detective, corrective, and governance controls support the same defined PII processing boundary.

42
00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:41.400
Reviewers should therefore ask for evidence at several layers: design records, configuration exports, change approvals, operational logs, test results, exception records, and corrective actions.

43
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:48.220
A technical safeguard is more credible when it has a living evidence trail across design, deployment, and operation.
